## What broke

`MfeHandlerMF` used `manifest.metaData.publicPath` exactly as the manifest gave it. The schema allows any string and the manifest type names `'/'` as a valid value, but only the stylesheet path (#661) resolved it against the document. Everything else concatenated the raw value:

- the chunk chain built `baseUrl + filename`, and the shared-dependency loader built `publicPath + chunkPath`, so with `'/'` or `mfe/x/` every fetch URL stayed relative;
- the relative chunk URL then reached the `import.meta.url` rewrite, so chunk code saw `import.meta.url` as `"/assets/"` and `new URL('./x', import.meta.url)` threw `Invalid URL` (asset URLs, workers, the Vite preload helper);
- a document-relative publicPath resolved against whatever the document URL happened to be at fetch or mount time, so it moved with client-side navigation.

The shared-dependency URL had its own problems: it was built by checking `chunkPath.startsWith('http')` and concatenating the rest, so `/shared/react.js` gave a double slash under the base, and a bare file name such as `http-helper.js` was taken for an absolute URL and fetched relative to the document. Two MFEs with different bases and the same such file name, without a content hash, then shared one cache key, so the second MFE received the first one's source.

This also contradicted the spec: mfe-isolation `inst-derive-url` says "derive the absolute chunk URL", and ADR 0034 keys fallback cache reuse on the absolute chunk URL.

## What changed

The handler resolves publicPath once per load, where the base URL is derived, and passes that base down to the shared-dependency fetch. The result is always a directory: a missing trailing `/` is added and a query or hash is dropped, so `https://cdn/mfe/x` and `https://cdn/mfe/x/` load the same files (before, the chunk chain produced `.../mfe/xassets/...` and shared dependencies lost the last segment). An empty value means the document's own directory (as `'./'` does), a relative value is resolved against `document.baseURI`, and an absolute value is parsed on its own without reading `document`. The resolved base must be an `http:` or `https:` URL (nothing in the spec allows another scheme). A value that is not a valid URL, resolves to another scheme (`data:`, `blob:`, `javascript:`, `file:`), or is relative where there is no document fails the load with `MfeLoadError` naming `manifest.metaData.publicPath` and the entry. Every URL built from it is absolute: chunk chain, shared dependencies, the `import.meta.url` rewrite and stylesheets. An absolute publicPath that already ends with `/` resolves to itself, so such manifests behave exactly as before. The `auto` placeholder is still rejected before resolution.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  P["manifest.metaData.publicPath<br/>'/' , 'mfe/x/' or absolute"] --> R["resolved once per load<br/>against document.baseURI"]
  R --> B["absolute base URL"]
  B --> C["chunk chain URLs"]
  B --> S["shared dependency URLs"]
  C --> M["import.meta.url rewrite<br/>(absolute chunk directory)"]
  B --> CSS["stylesheet hrefs"]
```

The shared-dependency loader receives that resolved base instead of reading the raw value again, so one load uses one base everywhere. Shared-dependency URLs are resolved against that base with `new URL`: an absolute `http(s)` chunkPath is used at its parsed form, so equivalent spellings share one fetch; any other path is resolved under the base and the resolved URL must still lie under it. Either way the URL must name a file: a value that resolves to a directory (`/`, `.`, `?v=1`, `#x`, `shared/`, or a URL ending in `/`) fails. A value that is not a string (an inline manifest skips the schema), a value with a space, a control character (C0, DEL or C1) or a backslash anywhere, a protocol-relative value, any other scheme (`data:`, `blob:`, `javascript:` and so on, also when hidden behind a leading `/` or whitespace) and a path that climbs out of the base with `..` all fail the load with `MfeLoadError` before anything is fetched. The check runs on the parsed URL, not the raw string, because the URL parser strips whitespace and control characters, so the no-hash fallback cache key is always an absolute URL unique to the MFE, as ADR 0034 states. The canonical rules for publicPath and chunkPath now live in the mfe-loading FEATURE (`inst-md-read-public-path`, `inst-md-resolve-chunk-path`), so a manifest generator outside GTS can find them. `MfManifestMetaData.publicPath` and `MfManifestShared.chunkPath` in `mf-manifest.ts` repeat them, and the gts-plugin `mf_manifest.v1.json` schema description points there. The flow steps for a malformed field and for the base URL (`inst-if-manifest-invalid`, `inst-manifest-invalid-fail`, `inst-derive-base-url`) are ticked and marked in the handler. The loader's private signature gains the base and entry arguments; the reflection helpers in `shared-dep-cache/cross-copy-boundary.test.ts` take the base from the handler's own `resolvePublicPath`.

A refused publicPath (including the `auto` placeholder) or chunkPath depends only on the manifest, so it is marked as a deterministic load failure (`inst-lto-if-deterministic-failure`, next to dependency cycles): the load rejects after one attempt instead of after about 3s and three attempts under the default retries.

cfs validate never read the mfe-loading FEATURE: its steps ended with an em dash, and the CDSL pattern needs a hyphen before `inst-...`. The separators are now hyphens. With the steps visible, cfs reports checked parents over pending steps, so the four parents whose own "Recorded debt" notes say their steps are ahead of the runtime (the on-demand load flow, manifest discovery, lazy-import ABI and the load lifecycle machine) are unchecked. No step changed state.

Docs: the `MfManifestMetaData.publicPath` comment now says relative values are resolved against the document base at load time, that an empty value means the document's own directory, and that a relative value needs a host document, and mfe-isolation `inst-derive-url` names the resolved absolute base.

`@gears-frontx/mfes` goes to 0.3.2 and `@gears-frontx/gts-plugin` to 0.3.3 (main already shipped gts-plugin 0.3.2 with #665), with the gts-plugin pin on mfes at 0.3.2.

## How it is proven

New tests in `MfeHandlerMF.test.ts` drive `handler.load()`; each fails on `main` or on the first version of this fix:

- one handler loads a manifest with publicPath `'/'` and then one with the absolute origin pointing at the same chunks: every chunk and the shared dependency is fetched exactly once, from absolute URLs;
- with publicPath `'/'`, the rewritten `import.meta.url` in the minted entry chunk is an absolute directory and `new URL('./x.svg', ...)` resolves (on `main`: `TypeError: Invalid URL`);
- with publicPath `mfe/x/`, a load at `/host/` fetches from `/host/mfe/x/...`, and after navigating to `/host/deep/route/page` the mounted stylesheet still points at the load-time base (on `main`: relative fetch URLs);
- with publicPath `''` and the document at `/host/page?q=1`, fetches go to `/host/...`, as a relative fetch did on `main`;
- a malformed value such as `'http://['` rejects with `MfeLoadError` naming the field and the entry, and nothing is fetched;
- without a `document`, an absolute publicPath still loads, `'/'` rejects with `MfeLoadError` before any fetch, and a malformed absolute value such as `'http://['` is reported as not a valid URL;
- shared-dependency chunkPath `shared/react.js`, `/shared/react.js`, `http-helper.js` and an absolute CDN URL all resolve to the expected absolute URL;
- two MFEs with different bases and the same `http-helper.js` without a content hash fetch their own copies instead of sharing one cache entry;
- a publicPath without a trailing `/`, with a query and hash, or document-relative without a slash loads every chunk, shared dependency and stylesheet from the same directory;
